 "youtube": {
  "title": "Pyrrhic Victory: How Did Winning Ruin a King?",
  "description": "A Pyrrhic victory is a win that costs too much, and the phrase has a real face. In 280 BC King Pyrrhus of Epirus crossed to Italy to fight Rome with war elephants no Roman army had ever met. At Heraclea the Roman horses panicked and he won; a year later at Asculum he won again. But his generals and veterans were dead and he had no one to replace them, while Rome simply raised new legions. Plutarch records his answer to a man who congratulated him: one more victory over the Romans, and we are utterly ruined.\n\nSubscribe to Stickstory for a true story like this every day. Can a victory ever be worth any price? Tell us in the comments!\n\n#shorts #pyrrhicvictory #ancientrome #stickstory",
